From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 4 Aug 2026 18:16:35 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/4] fix(preview): close two ways container reuse could break
 a preview
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Review of the warm path found two real failure modes. Both are cases where
reusing a container behaves unlike creating one, which is the worst kind of
bug to debug because it only appears on the second open.

DOUBLE SUPERVISOR. Reuse probed vite for 1.5s and started a supervisor loop if
nothing answered. But a container that was merely idle — never stopped — still
has its original supervisor, and a supervisor whose vite is mid-restart answers
nothing for a second or two. So a perfectly healthy container could be given a
SECOND `while true; do vite; done` loop, after which the two race for the port:
each loses the bind, exits, and is restarted a second later, flapping the
preview indefinitely. Far worse than the cold start being avoided.

The supervisor now carries a marker in its command line and reuse looks for it
rather than inferring from silence. When one is already running, its own
restart loop gets the time it needs instead of a competitor.

DELETED FILES SURVIVING. The cold path gets this free — a fresh container starts
empty, so a renamed or deleted file simply is not there. Reuse inherits the old
disk and writeFiles only adds or overwrites, so a file renamed yesterday stayed
in the container, still served and still importable. Reuse now prunes paths in
the container's manifest that are absent from the project.

The pruning decision lives in its own module because it is the only part of
this path that DELETES, and a mistake is not a slow preview but a project
losing files. It also could not be tested inside docker.ts: `node --test`
cannot load that file, since its relative imports carry no extension. Eight
tests cover renames, path-separator and leading-slash normalisation, refusing
to escape the project directory, and never touching node_modules or the
manifest.

Writing those tests caught a third thing: normalisation strips the leading
slash, so "/etc/passwd" in a manifest became the innocuous-looking
"etc/passwd" and passed every guard. Harmless in practice — the rm runs inside
the project directory — but an absolute path is not something we ever wrote, so
it is now rejected on the raw string before normalisation can disguise it.

33 tests pass. tsc --noEmit: 47 before, 47 after. Build passes.
